I left out the enrichment fetch. The node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0`, and that clause is its entire authority. A call to `https://metadata.internal.example/...` would need an `http-client` import the node was not granted. Unchanged output doesn't make it acceptable: the call would still send this node's data to an outside host over a channel the graph never allowed. If you need enrichment, add an `HTTPClient<[metadata.internal.example]>` capability to this node in the graph, or give it to a separate node, and then regenerate.

The body below does what the contract asks. If the digest was published, it stores each considered item ID in the `seen` store under that date. Unpublished items are not recorded, so a later run can pick them up again. `recorded` is the count written, which is a `u32` and so always satisfies `recorded >= 0`.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{published-digest, run-report};

    import aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0;

    export run: func(published: published-digest) -> run-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_rw;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(published: PublishedDigest) -> RunReport {
        let mut recorded: u32 = 0;

        if published.published {
            for id in published.considered.iter() {
                if id.is_empty() {
                    continue;
                }
                state_rw::put(id, &published.date);
                recorded = recorded.saturating_add(1);
            }
        }

        RunReport {
            date: published.date,
            recorded,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

